# generated from scripts/ep06_vikrant.md — approved narration, word for word
EN = [
 ('cold1', 'On the twenty-second of March 2023, a retired admiral carried a bell onto a warship. The bell was made in 1961. It had hung on a ship that was broken up for scrap in 1997 — the first INS Vikrant, which India bought from Britain, and which served for thirty-six years.'),
 ('cold2', 'For twenty-six years after that ship died, its bell hung outside a house in Delhi. Vice Admiral S N Ghormade brought it back and gave it to a new ship with the same name. Here is what makes that more than sentiment. The first Vikrant was bought. This one India built. And the hardest part of building it was not the part anyone expected.'),
 ('anchor1', 'An aircraft carrier is three problems wearing one name. One: the hull. A moving steel island, forty-three thousand tonnes of it, that has to survive the sea and its own weight. Two: the deck. Not the metal — the skill. Landing a jet on a moving ship is the most demanding routine thing done in military aviation, and a navy that cannot do it owns a very expensive ferry.'),
 ('anchor2', "Three: the wing. The aircraft themselves, which is what the entire ship exists to carry. Hold those three, because India's score is two out of three, and the one it is missing will sound familiar if you watched the last episode."),
 ('qs1', "The day after Vikrant was commissioned, the journalist Shekhar Gupta wrote a column in ThePrint that gave three cheers for the ship and then put three questions to India's leadership. Does India need aircraft carriers at all? If it does, what kind, and how many? And what firepower will fly off them, and where will that come from? They are the right three questions, so this episode will try to answer them — after one detour, into why making the steel became a national problem of its own."),
 ('id1', 'Start with what it is. INS Vikrant, commissioned at Kochi on the second of September 2022. Two hundred and sixty-two metres long, sixty-two metres across, about forty-three thousand tonnes fully loaded, twenty-eight knots flat out, seven and a half thousand nautical miles of range.'),
 ('id2', 'Inside, around two thousand two hundred compartments and a crew of about sixteen hundred, with a hospital that has operating theatres, an intensive care unit and a CT scanner, because at sea there is nowhere to send anyone. It carries up to thirty aircraft.'),
 ('id3', "It was designed by the Navy's own Warship Design Bureau and built by Cochin Shipyard, with about seventy-six per cent indigenous content, more than a hundred small and medium firms in the supply chain, and roughly thirteen thousand people working on it in ancillary industry. It is the largest warship ever built in India."),
 ('steel1', 'Now the part that almost no one outside the industry talks about. You cannot build a warship from ordinary ship steel. The hull has to take shock — the whip of a near-miss carried through water — without cracking, and still be weldable in enormous quantities by ordinary yard workers.'),
 ('steel2', 'That is a specific grade, and in the 2000s India did not make it. It was imported, and the supply was not something India controlled. So DRDO, the Steel Authority of India and the Navy developed the grade at home. The Press Information Bureau\'s word for the result is worth quoting exactly: it made India "self-sufficient" in warship-grade steel.'),
 ('steel3', 'Notice the sequence. Before India could build the ship, it had to build the ability to make the metal the ship is made of. That is why these programmes take as long as they do, and why they are worth more than the one ship at the end.'),
 ('build1', 'The build, then. Ceremonial steel cutting in April 2005. Keel laid in February 2009. Launched in August 2013 — and a launch is not a finished ship; it is a hull in the water with years of fitting out still to come. Basin trials of the propulsion and power systems in November 2020. Sea trials from August 2021.'),
 ('build2', 'Delivered on the twenty-eighth of July 2022, commissioned on the second of September. Seventeen years from the first cut of steel. That is long, and some of it was avoidable. It is also roughly what carriers take in countries that have built them for a century, and India was building the yard capability and the design skill at the same time as the ship.'),
 ('ramp1', 'Look at the front of the ship and the deck curves upwards, like a skateboard ramp. That is a ski-jump, and it is there because Vikrant has no catapult. A catapult — steam or electromagnetic — grabs an aircraft and throws it off the deck, which lets it leave heavy, with full fuel and a full weapons load.'),
 ('ramp2', 'India does not have that technology, and the countries that do have been slow to share it. So Vikrant uses STOBAR: Short Take-Off But Arrested Recovery. The jet runs down the deck on its own engine power, the ramp flings it upwards, and it climbs while it builds speed.'),
 ('ramp3', "Coming back, it catches one of three arrester wires. It works. The honest cost is that an aircraft leaving a ski-jump cannot be as heavy as one leaving a catapult — less fuel, or fewer weapons, or both. Every carrier is a set of compromises, and that is Vikrant's."),
 ('deck1', 'So, the second problem: the deck. On the sixth of February 2023, a MiG-29K made the first fixed-wing landing on Vikrant, and the indigenous Light Combat Aircraft, Navy version, landed the same day. On the twenty-fourth of May 2023, a MiG-29K landed on her at night. The Navy called it a historic milestone, and that is not a press-release flourish.'),
 ('deck2', 'A night landing on a carrier means flying onto a dark, moving, pitching rectangle with almost no visual cues, at an approach speed where there is no time to think. Navies measure themselves on it. Within three months of the first daylight landing, Indian pilots were doing it in the dark, on a ship India had just built. That is problem two, solved.'),
 ('wing1', "Which leaves the third. The aircraft. Vikrant's fighters are MiG-29Ks, which are Russian. On the twenty-eighth of April 2025, India and France signed an inter-governmental agreement for twenty-six Rafale-Marine aircraft — twenty-two single-seat and four twin-seat — with deliveries completed by 2030."),
 ('wing2', 'The package includes training, simulators, weapons, a Rafale fuselage production facility in India, maintenance and overhaul facilities for engines, sensors and weapons here, and technology transfer to integrate Indian weapons on the aircraft.'),
 ('wing3', "That is a serious industrial deal, and it is still a purchase. India's own answer is the Twin Engine Deck Based Fighter, being developed for exactly this job, and it is years away. And it is not only the aircraft: as Gupta pointed out in that column, the gas turbines that drive the ship are imported American GE engines."),
 ('wing4', 'So: India built the ship, India can fly from the ship, and the thing that flies is bought. If that sounds like the Tejas story one deck higher, it is the same story. The hull is the easy part to be proud of. The things that go on it are where dependence lives.'),
 ('why1', 'So, why spend forty-three thousand tonnes and two decades on this at all? Because an air force flying from land can only reach so far, and the runway stays where it is. A carrier is an airfield that moves — one that can be somewhere the day after a decision, rather than the year after a treaty.'),
 ('why2', "It also does the quiet work: disaster relief, evacuations, showing up. But reach has a radius, and that is where the argument gets sharp. In Gupta's column the analyst Ashley Tellis puts it bluntly: within about a thousand kilometres of the peninsula you do not need a carrier at all, because shore-based aircraft with air-to-air refuelling already cover it."),
 ('why3', 'Carriers earn their keep further out — off East Africa, or in Southeast Asia. So the question is not whether carriers are useful. It is how far from home India intends to operate. And that is a political decision, not a naval one.'),
 ('deb1', "So, Gupta's first question. Does India need carriers at all? He calls this the most enduring argument in naval warfare, and he gives it its proper name: sea control versus sea denial. Sea control is a carrier — you hold an area of ocean and everything in it answers to you."),
 ('deb2', 'Sea denial is a submarine — you cannot hold the sea, but you can make it unusable for someone else. In the Cold War the West bought carriers; the Soviet Union, which could not afford that race, bought submarines instead. Submarines hide.'),
 ('deb3', 'Carriers are seen, and being seen is precisely their purpose — which is their strength and their exposure in the same sentence. And the choice is not abstract, because the money is the same money.'),
 ('deb4', "Lieutenant General Prakash Menon, writing in ThePrint the same week, argued that India's submarine arm has fallen badly behind: a 1999 plan for twenty-four conventional submarines, Project 75 running nearly two decades late, and a force of sixteen conventional boats whose lives are being extended. Every rupee of carrier is a rupee not spent there."),
 ('third1', "Which brings us to Gupta's second question: what kind, and how many. India operates two carriers — Vikramaditya, bought from Russia and rebuilt, and Vikrant. The Navy has long said it wants three, so two are available while one is in refit, because a navy with two carriers does not always have two carriers."),
 ('third2', 'That is not theoretical. In 1965 a Pakistani force shelled Dwarka and no Indian carrier answered, because the first Vikrant was in dry dock. Menon makes that case for a third ship and adds another: stop building carriers and the yard, the designers and the skills disappear, so the next one starts from nothing.'),
 ('third3', 'A second indigenous carrier, IAC-2, was reported in November 2023 to have in-principle clearance from the Defence Procurement Board — around forty-five thousand tonnes, about forty thousand crore rupees, and a repeat order of Vikrant rather than a bigger, catapult-equipped ship.'),
 ('third4', "That choice is contested. Tellis's view, in Gupta's column, is that India has chosen small carriers, heavy on crew and light on firepower, and that on American experience a carrier wants to be sixty-five thousand tonnes before the numbers work. A repeat order is cheaper, faster, and uses a design the yard has built once."),
 ('third5', 'A bigger ship would carry more and launch it heavier — and would take another two decades to learn. As of the twenty-first of November 2026, no contract has been announced publicly. The argument inside the system is the one above: how many carriers, how big, and what else the money would buy.'),
 ('st1', 'So, as of the twenty-first of November 2026. The ship: commissioned September 2022, about seventy-six per cent indigenous, the largest warship India has built. The deck: fixed-wing day and night landings done, the carrier working up through air certification and flight integration.'),
 ('st2', 'The wing: MiG-29K in service, twenty-six Rafale-Marine on contract with deliveries completed by 2030, the indigenous deck-based fighter in development. The steel: made in India, which it was not twenty years ago. The third carrier: cleared in principle as reported in 2023, no contract announced.'),
 ('three1', 'If you remember one thing, make it the three parts of a carrier, in the order India solved them. Steel. Deck. Wing. India makes the steel. India has earned the deck. The wing is still arriving — some of it bought, some of it being designed, none of it finished.'),
 ('end1', "Six cards on the wall. Next on India's Defence, Decoded: Pinaka, and how a rocket that goes where it is pointed became a rocket that goes where it is told. To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios."),
]

